today in the case of DC versus Heller  the Supreme Court for the very first  time in US history  authoritative Lee declared that the  Second Amendment to the Constitution  does indeed protect an individual right  to own weapons for self-defense in the  home that right is not necessarily  connected to membership in any kind of  organized militia gun control forces of  for decades been arguing that it is not  an individual right the Supreme Court  has now declared that it is  this decision was a very important  victory in the long-lasting war over gun  control and gun rights but it is by no  means the end of the war the Supreme  Court made a valuable and desirable but  very limited decision in in DC v Heller  the the laws that were being challenged  in this case were the worst most  draconian anti gun laws that this  country has ever known in in DC since  the 1976 it has been completely illegal  to have a handgun of any sort and while  it's been legal to have a rifle or a  shotgun you had to keep them  disassembled and/or unloaded in trigger  locked which meant it was never illegal  to have a functioning weapon in your  home for self defense the Supreme Court  has said that that will not fly that  kind of law is not legitimate under the  Second Amendment however there are all  sorts of other gun control regulations  that will continue to be legitimate some  of which the court actually listed in in  their decision including  laws regulating concealed carry laws  regulating the type of people who are  permitted to own guns for example a  felons are not legally allowed to own  guns that's still okay under their  decision  people have been institutionalized  that's still okay so they have not gone  ahead and said authoritative ly what  sort of gun control regulations might be  vulnerable under their decision except  that a complete ban is now off the table  gun control groups interestingly enough  have been trying to spin the Heller  decision as a victory of sorts for them  and their thinking is pretty interesting  they're maintaining that for four  decades the gun rights Lobby the NRA and  other groups of that sort have made  their bread and butter on this fear that  someday a federal agent is going to come  knocking on your door trying to take  your guns away and that gun rights  people have seen every single gun  regulation as just a step on that  slippery slope toward a total ban toward  total confiscation what the hell're  decision is done it's taken the ban  issue off the table the Supreme Court  has said that states localities the  federal government might be legally able  to regulate gun ownership in many many  ways but one thing they cannot do is  completely ban having the use of guns in  your home so with that issue off the  table there's still an enormous amount  of room for challenges to all sorts of  gun regulations from concealed carry  regulations from regulations affecting  gun dealers and we are definitely going  to see tons and tons more lawsuits about  gun regulations down the line this is by  no means the end of the public policy  fight over gun control it by no means  the end of the legal fight over gun  control like with most major Supreme  Court decisions this is going to be the  beginning of decades of more lawsuits  decades and more legal decisions decades  more debates in in you know state houses  City Council's across the country but  the guidance that the Supreme Court is  given today is that we do need to  remember that we do as Americans have  the right to self-defense and that guns  weapons is as unpleasant as many people  find them are are often essential tools  toward that self-defense and  self-defense is a right not created by  the Second Amendment it's a right we  have inherently as human beings and it's  a right that's protected by the Second  Amendment and that's a very important  victory for for the cause of gun rights  and human rights in America  for many many decades every single  federal appeals court except one the  Fifth Circuit has fallen into this  pretty unsupportable view that there is  no individual right to own weapons that  because the word militia is in is in the  Second Amendment that it only applies to  membership in a militia there was never  any good reason to believe this and if  you read Justice Scalia's opinion he  really tears it apart piece by piece in  a very entertaining way sometimes having  to say things that you're amazed that  they had to be said in a Supreme Court  opinion for example that the word keep  as in keep arms means that someone can  actually keep a gun in their home  strangely enough lots of very bright  legal minds have have argued otherwise  for decades and a Scalia finally slapped  that nonsense down so yeah the  establishment that there is an  individual right even though that right  may be regulatable in many ways that  were only going to figure out with  future court decisions is a very good  base from which to start and if the  decision had come down the other way and  it was a 5-4 decision it would very  easily could have that would be the end  basically any the end for gun rights  basically any locality anywhere could  completely eliminate our ability to  defend ourselves in the best way  possible so absolutely a very good and a  vitally important decision but not at  all the final word in the fight over a  gun gun regulation
 

